\begin_layout Standard
In deriving the newton's third equation by graph, two values are taken from
 the graph
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $a=\dfrac{v-u}{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s=\dfrac{v+u}{2}t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Multiplying both the equations,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $as=\dfrac{v^{2}-u{}^{2}}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OR
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $v^{2}-u{}^{2}=2as$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.
 Result from Area Under the v-t graph, 
\begin_inset Formula $s=v_{av}t=\dfrac{v+u}{2}t$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.
 Manipulating 
\begin_inset Formula $v=u+at$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $u=v-at$
\end_inset

 and substituting in 
\begin_inset Formula $s=\dfrac{v+u}{2}t$
\end_inset

 gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $s=\dfrac{v-at+v}{2}t$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow s=vt-\dfrac{1}{2}at^{2}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

